rishi rambaran's kids were 11 9 and 5  years old when the pandemic began and  mandatory masking first came into effect  initially it was cool we got some pretty  cool masks they they liked it two years  later it's largely a non-issue still the  winnipeg dad wonders if masking is  making some things harder for his kids  you know humans we thrive on on  interaction so much of that is facial  recognition and playing off each other's  faces psychology professor michelle  morningstar says luckily humans  interpret emotions other ways children  do have access to all these other cues  that they use to infer other people's  emotions there's tone and body language  a study from late 2020 had kids look at  pictures of people either with masks on  sunglasses on or unobstructed the  results masks didn't negatively impact  ability to read emotions any more than  sunglasses did right now there are no  data to suggest that there are any  short-term or long-term harms when it  comes to masks in kids and that includes  both psychological harms and physical  harms at the hannan center in toronto  they specialize in speech and language  development our educators are masked in  an early childhood setting we're masked  out in public but they're not masked at  home so hopefully children are still  getting you know lots of good  interactions from parents at home  director janice greenberg says children  who suffer from a speech delay may be  disproportionately affected but there  are ways to foster these skills with a  mask on speak loudly stand closer to a  child and verbalize your emotion  rambaran's eldest krishana is now 14. it  bogged us a little but other than that  it was pretty  regular a sign of just how adaptable  kids can be giving experts hope any  potential impacts won't last once our  masks come off for good  jamie morocco global news toronto
 

